<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lys (department)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lys was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the French Republic and Empire, formed in 1795 after the annexation of the Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Named after the river Lys, it covered the western part of the former County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, and its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s fall in 1814 it became the province of West Flanders, under the Netherlands and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lys-department.jpg" alt="Lys (department)" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Excommunicato (original version by Naturals) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lys_departement_(1812).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
